      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "The Against Malaria Foundation demonstrates a unique financial model where <cite id=\"3\">100.0% of public donations</cite> are used to buy nets. This is supported by a <cite id=\"7\">$0.00 fundraising efficiency</cite> metric, which is rare in the nonprofit sector. Their operational transparency is further validated by a <cite id=\"10\">Platinum Seal of Transparency from Candid</cite>, the highest level of disclosure available. Additionally, the organization has <cite id=\"11\">documented its theory of change</cite> and tracks <cite id=\"12\">two specific outcome metrics</cite> to ensure that the nets distributed are actually protecting people from malaria."
      ],
      "summary": "The Against Malaria Foundation (AMF) is a specialized humanitarian organization focused on the distribution of long-lasting insecticidal nets (LLINs) to combat malaria in regions across Africa, Asia, and South America. In FY2025, the organization reported <cite id=\"1\">total revenue of $62,286,888</cite>, a figure that reflects a significant <cite id=\"2\">three-year revenue growth rate of 80.7%</cite>. Despite this financial scale, the organization currently holds a Below Average assessment band due to critical gaps in financial health metrics and a lack of alignment with specific faith-based donor requirements. While AMF maintains a <cite id=\"3\">100.0% program expense ratio</cite>, meaning every dollar of public donation is directed toward net procurement and distribution, its <cite id=\"4\">working capital ratio stands at 0.0 months</cite>. This indicates the charity lacks the liquid reserves typically required to sustain operations during funding gaps. The charity's governance is also noted as minimal, with a <cite id=\"6\">board size of only four members</cite>, which is below the standard for an organization managing over $60 million in annual contributions.",
